# This file includes the list of commits to exclude from `git blame`.
# These are chores, like upgrading copyrights, or running any kind of automated tools like black, isort, rubocop, etc.
#
# How-to:
# * Use only full 40-character hashes
# * Here's an example of a way to grep the commit history to find commits to exclude:
# ```
# git log --all --reverse --grep='copyright' --grep='license' --grep='\[chore\]' --shortstat --pretty='format:# %C(auto)%s [%an, %as]%n%H%n' >> .git-blame-ignore-revs
# Add commits from the style bot
# git log --all --reverse --shortstat --pretty='format:# %C(auto)%s [%an, %as]%n%H%n' --author='energyplus-style-bot' >> .git-blame-ignore-revs
# ```
# You can add `--shortstat` to it to see the number of files changes, then do a replace `:%s/\n \(files\? changed.*\)/#    \1/g`
#
# To add the last commit:
# ```
# echo '' >> .git-blame-ignore-revs
# git log -n1 --pretty='format:# %C(auto)%s [%an, %as]%n%H%n' >> .git-blame-ignore-revs
# ```
#
# Note: On older git (like 2.17 which is default in 18.04, without `sudo add-apt-repository ppa:git-core/ppa`),
# '%as' (date in YYYY-MM-DD)  doesn't work, use '%ai' instead
#
# * Locally, you can do `$ git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs`
# * Add to the bottom of the list, so we keep everything in chronological order (oldest to newest)

